column1:
Labubu toys, the grinning fuzzy trinkets that became a global sensation last year, are going to be the stars of a Hollywood movie, joining a growing list of beloved characters to jump from store shelves to the big screen. (Hi, Barbie!) Sony and the Chinese retailer Pop Mart, which exclusively sells the dolls, announced on Thursday that they were developing a Labubu feature film. Paul King, the British screenwriter and director behind {LQ}Paddington{RQ} and {LQ}Wonka,{RQ} will produce and direct the film, according to a statement from the companies. King will collaborate with Steven Levenson, who wrote the book for the musical {LQ}Dear Evan Hansen,{RQ} on the screenplay. The announcement offered few details and did not give a release date. But the film will become the latest to turn beloved toys into the stars of a major movie, along with Barbie, Lego, the Transformers and others. The close link between movies and merchandising is often traced back to the immense success of the original {LQ}Star Wars{RQ} trilogy in the late 1970s and early ’80s, intellectual property experts said. {LQ}It took seriously the idea of your intellectual property being commercialized on quite a few different channels,{RQ} Emily Hudson, a professor of law at the University of Oxford, said of {LQ}Star Wars.{RQ} But whereas {LQ}Star Wars{RQ} started as a movie and became a major merchandising success, that model has been inverted lately. {LQ}Now you come up first with the merchandise and then you retrofit some sort of cinematic universe or some television show around the toys,{RQ} said Luke McDonagh, a professor at the London School of Economics who researches intellectual property law.

That strategy has paid huge dividends. {LQ}The Lego Movie,{RQ} made by Warner Bros. and the Lego Group, took in nearly $500 million at the global box office in 2014, and was followed by a sequel and two spinoffs. Paramount Pictures and Hasbro have turned the Transformers action-figure line into a $5 billion big-screen franchise. The toy company Mattel has moved to turn its brands into full-fledged entertainment brands, including {LQ}Barbie,{RQ} the live-action adventure starring Margot Robbie that earned nearly $1.5 billion.

A {LQ}Polly Pocket{RQ} movie, co-produced by Reese Witherspoon’s production company, is in the works. {LQ}Characters are now the most valuable kind of intellectual property,{RQ} said Dev Gangjee, a professor of intellectual property law at the University of Oxford. {LQ}They straddle all these formats.{RQ}
